Z||000000||RobertVol||04-18-2000||07:09 PM||||I have picked up a 88 GT to go with my 88 White 3.8 GEN II SC T topper. NOW I want to try another route with this one. I would like to know if I want to just mod the Stock engine. How much would it cost to stroke this engine to say get about 200BHP? What parts would be needed? Cost ? I was looking to spend about $800 - $1500. All Suggestions would be appreciated. Of course being a Scummy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> office worker I would have to pay someone to do this, so labor cost would have to included I guess....||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||04-18-2000||09:47 PM||||Have you sent the numbers to 88 nut?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||04-18-2000||10:59 PM||||Don't expect 200 horsepower from a stroked 2.8.  I'm about 175 or so.  To get close you'd need a really big cam and some custom high compression pistons.  My car runs okay, I'm just learning to settle with marginally better.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RobertVol||04-18-2000||11:12 PM||||I have been told you can get to 190 - 200. .......Uh 88 nut ?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||RobertVol||04-18-2000||11:49 PM||||What about the 899.00 performance overhaul package to Fierostore sells with the Comp cams .anyone every purchased it ? does it give any performance upgrade ? If so how much ? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||04-19-2000||09:22 AM||||Tom is reffering to 88DatabaseNut.  He is collecting as much information as possible about the whereabouts and options, mods, etc on the 88 fieros.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||pollock||04-19-2000||09:32 AM||||With the money you're wanting to spend, there's only one way to get 200 hp.  Nitrous.<P>I haven't priced NOS for a Fiero, but kits for most cars run in the $400-500 range.  And that's for parts in a box sitting on the floor.  Your budget certainly should afford installation.<P>I understand that the only NOS kit available for Fieros is a 70hp shot.  <P>1FST2M6, a forum member, should be a good source of info on this.  I believe he runs a much bigger shot than that.||||reg||4
Z||000007||1FST2M6||04-20-2000||08:30 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg</A>    I'm running with 96,000 miles and have 155hp at the crank. TOTALY stock! (MOTOR, many bolt-ons) I'm sure with a decent valve job a nice cam and the right tuning you'll be FINE with the 200HP number your looking for. Get a decent set of pistons! I'm not trying to start a riot again... but nice FORGED not Hypereutectic. Hp numbers don't mean a whole lot... Torque numbers are what make your squeeze your cheeks and be affraid. I've got about 202lbft at the crank on my STOCK motor. and 310lbft at the flywheel under nitrous... I've got a set of Chris West headers but they have NOT been installed yet. I'll be sure to post their results up here.  For more info on why not to buy Hyper- piston see the book "How to build and tune your Nitrous charged motor" available at barnes and noble  <A HREF="http://www.bn.com" TARGET=_blank>www.bn.com</A>   ($16.95) Their great for S/C or a turbo motor.  (I got my 27.5% Drive line power loss from the local Pontiac dealer; Carl Black-Kennesaw,Ga.)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||04-20-2000||09:15 AM||||I wouldn't even say hyper pistons are great for supercharged and turbo applications.  They're good for a naturally aspirated motor that isn't making too much power.  All hyper pistons are different.  Some can withstand much more abuse than others ,for instance, the stock mustang 5.0 pistons.  People are known to use the stock shortblock to 600 hp without any meltdowns under supercharged and nitrous use.  Would shy away from using them on a blown Fiero motor though until I know they are safe.||||reg||1
Z||000009||RobertVol||04-21-2000||01:54 AM||||Hey Ifst2m6.....what have you done so far to get to 155hp? Also good to hear that from. I just could not believe that it can not be done. How much do you think that job would run ? cost wise ? <P>The reason I am asking and insist to stay with the motor I have is money is not the real problem. if it costs ilittle more fine but I wouold like to see over a period time how much you could "SQUEEZE" out of the little 2.8. I personally Like the little engine. And think its more durable than alot of people think. I have had three GT's And the 2st one had was when I was just a young 'en and it was My first car when 16. No what happens when you mix that ..? usually a dogged car. BUT that  little 87 GT just kept going and going all the way to 100K miles and no problems other than reg work. And I admit it I was yooung and I dogged it! and it took it and I LOved it. so The 2.8 is not a bad engine to work with in my opinion. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||RobertVol||04-21-2000||01:57 AM||||Err must be late bad typing ...The 1ST GT I HAD!! not 2Nd ....Man I need to smoke another !!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||04-21-2000||10:43 AM||||most major mod was Exhaust... 2.5" Random Technologys cat, into a single out 2.5" 2 chamber flowmaster, into a 4" stainless 5Zidgen tip. (NOT ricey and lame)  I had a Crane HI-6s but sold it and got an MSD box... it's a 6BTM. in anticipation of the S/C install. K&N filter... for a better list look here...  <A HREF="http://www.freeyellow.com/members/fiero/travis.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.freeyellow.com/members/fiero/travis.html</A>     or   <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net/fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net/fiero.htm</A> ||||reg||1
